<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- The app declares **one** permission of its own, and requests **no
         INTERNET permission**, so it cannot open a socket: §1's "offline-first,
         verifiably" holds in the way that matters.

         What is here, and why the merged manifest is the thing to read rather
         than this file:

         - ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is REMOVED below. Jetpack Glance requires
           WorkManager (it runs its composition session in a CoroutineWorker),
           and WorkManager's manifest asks for it in order to evaluate network
           constraints on work requests. Nothing in this app has one. Keeping it
           would falsify PRD §5 and §7's "no network permission at MVP", which
           is a headline claim, for a capability no code here uses.
         - WAKE_LOCK, RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED and FOREGROUND_SERVICE arrive with
           WorkManager and are KEPT, deliberately: WorkManager genuinely wakes
           and reschedules, so removing them would be a manifest that lies about
           what the app does. None of them can move data off the device.
         - DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION is androidx.core's own
           signature-level permission for registering a non-exported dynamic
           receiver. It predates the widget and grants nothing to anything else.
         - POST_NOTIFICATIONS is the app's own, and the only one declared by hand
           here. The end-of-day reminder (PRD §4) is a notification, so there is
           no version of that feature without it. It is a **runtime** permission
           on API 33+, requested from the settings screen's reminder row rather
           than at first launch, and it grants nothing but the ability to put a
           row in the user's own shade. docs/ux/reminder.md §3 has the argument.

         ManifestPermissionTest asserts this exact set, so anything arriving
         through any library's manifest fails a test rather than being noticed
         later. docs/ux/widget.md §5 has the measurement.

         allowBackup is off because Android Auto Backup would copy app data to
         the user's Google account without any network permission
         (architecture §6). -->

    <!-- Not "unused": measured. See the comment above. If a future WorkRequest
         ever takes a network constraint, this line is what has to go first —
         and the reminder's two WorkRequests deliberately carry no constraint at
         all, which ReminderScheduler.enqueue's KDoc records as a rule rather
         than an omission. -->
    <uses-permission
        android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE"
        tools:node="remove" />

    <!-- The reminder. Runtime-requested on API 33+; below that the grant is
         implicit and the user's switch in system settings is the only control,
         which is why the settings row reads NotificationManagerCompat rather
         than checkSelfPermission. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!-- android:icon below is Android's generic default, and a placeholder on
         purpose rather than an oversight: there is no mipmap/ic_launcher in this
         app at all. A real icon is the same decision as Momo's art style, so it
         waits on PRD OQ-4 — a mark drawn before the character is settled would
         be redrawn to match it. PRD §5's Phase 1 records this next to the
         mascot, along with why UI polish waits on the same thing.

         sym_def_app_icon is not public API. It has been stable for years, so
         this is not urgent, but the fix is to replace it rather than to lean on
         it further. -->
    <application
        android:name=".GawiApplication"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/full_backup_content"
        android:icon="@android:drawable/sym_def_app_icon"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Gawi">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>

</manifest>
